---
// Methodology / about page (Gate 11, 2026-07-31). Describes only what the
// project actually does today — CLAUDE.md rule 6. Operator outreach (Gate 8)
// has not run yet, so this page does NOT claim operator verification; every
// fact is currently "published by the venue," checked against the venue's own
// materials, with operator confirmation named as a future step, not a present
// one.
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { SITE_NAME } from "../../config";

const breadcrumbLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  itemListElement: [
    { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: new URL("/", Astro.site).href },
    { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "How this directory is made", item: new URL("/methodology/", Astro.site).href },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`How this directory is made — ${SITE_NAME}`}
  description="How Where We Bathe documents Australian bathhouses and hot springs: sourced from each venue's own materials, checked by hand, with a per-field record of where every fact came from."
  canonicalPath="/methodology/"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbLd)} />
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-16 md:py-24">
    <header class="mb-12">
      <h1 class="font-display font-light" style="font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);">How this directory is made</h1>
    </header>

    <section class="reading-spine">
      <p>
        Where We Bathe is a free, ad-free directory of Australian venues built around heat, cold and water —
        bathhouses, saunas, hot springs and mineral pools. No venue can pay for a listing, for placement, or for a
        higher ranking. There are no sponsored entries and no advertising anywhere on the site.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-display font-light mt-12" style="font-size: 1.75rem;">Where the facts come from</h2>
      <p>
        Each entry starts as the venue's own published account — its website, its own descriptions of its pools,
        saunas, temperatures, hours and prices. That material is read, drafted into a consistent entry, and then
        checked line by line before publication: every specific figure has to trace back to something the venue
        itself has published. Nothing is invented to fill a gap. Where a venue doesn't state something, the entry
        simply leaves it out rather than guessing.
      </p>
      <p>
        We do not write about visits we haven't made. You won't find first-person "on arrival" impressions or
        invented sensory detail in these entries — only what a venue documents about itself, tidied into a form
        that's quick to read and easy to compare.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-display font-light mt-12" style="font-size: 1.75rem;">How each fact is tracked</h2>
      <p>
        Every factual claim an entry makes — price, hours, temperatures, dress code, session policies — carries a
        record of where it came from and how strongly it's verified. Today, that record is honest about its limits:
        almost every fact is marked <em>published by the venue</em>, meaning it's drawn from the venue's own current
        materials and checked, but not yet independently confirmed with the operator.
      </p>
      <p>
        The next step, which is underway, is operator outreach: contacting venues directly to confirm the details
        we hold and correct anything out of date. As a venue confirms its facts, those move to a stronger
        <em>confirmed by the operator</em> mark. We'd rather tell you exactly how sure we are than imply a
        certainty we don't have.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-display font-light mt-12" style="font-size: 1.75rem;">Structured, comparable data</h2>
      <p>
        Behind each entry is a structured record — adult drop-in price, sauna and plunge temperatures where the
        venue states them, drive time from the nearest capital, and the amenities and facilities on offer. That's
        what makes the <a href="/compare/">side-by-side comparisons</a> and <a href="/region/">regional roundups</a>
        possible, and it's what lets an AI assistant cite a specific, sourced figure rather than a vague summary.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-display font-light mt-12" style="font-size: 1.75rem;">Coverage</h2>
      <p>
        Coverage is strongest in Victoria for now, with a growing presence in Tasmania, New South Wales,
        Queensland and, most recently, South Australia, Western Australia, the Northern Territory and the ACT. The
        directory is added to steadily; a thin region reflects where the work has reached, not a judgement about
        what's worth visiting.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-12 mono text-[var(--ink-faded)]">
        Found something out of date? <a href={`/`}>Every venue page</a> has a "claim this listing" link for
        operators, and corrections are always welcome.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
